<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Whitby Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">657</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Streoneshalh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Whitby Abbey was a monastery on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a> coast founded in 657 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu of Northumbria</a>, with Abbess Hild ruling a double house of monks and nuns. In 664 it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-whitby">Synod of Whitby</a>, at which Oswiu chose Roman over Irish practice for dating Easter. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedmon">Cædmon</a> was a lay brother there. Refounded as a Benedictine abbey after the Norman Conquest, it was dissolved in 1539.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/whitby-abbey.jpg" alt="Whitby Abbey" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Buck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Whitby_Abbey_(geograph_7249897).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
